# For working on Firetower from a checkout. Copy to .env and fill in what you
# need — every value here has a working default, which is the difference
# between this file and deploy/.env.example, where three of them have none.
#
# Running Firetower rather than building it uses that other one. See the README.
#
# Firetower reads ./.env, then ~/.firetower/.env.
#
# Nothing here is a credential. Those go in the secret store, encrypted — so if
# you ever find yourself putting one here, something has gone wrong.

# Authorizing GitHub is no longer configured here — the interface asks for a
# client id in the setup wizard, and again on the connect-a-repository screen,
# and keeps it in the database.

# The control plane's database. `just dev` starts one from compose.yaml;
# this is where to point it somewhere else.
# DATABASE_URL=postgres://firetower:firetower@localhost:5433/firetower

# What compose creates the database with.
# POSTGRES_USER=firetower
# POSTGRES_PASSWORD=firetower
# POSTGRES_DB=firetower
# POSTGRES_PORT=5433

# The host address compose publishes the database on. Defaults to 127.0.0.1,
# so it is reachable from this machine and not from the network it is on —
# which is what you want for a database that comes up with the credentials
# above, the same two words in every checkout.
#
# It has its own variable rather than following HOST_BIND below,
# so that opening the stack up to a laptop does not open the database up with
# it. Set it only for a database client on another machine.
# POSTGRES_BIND=127.0.0.1

# The key every stored credential is encrypted with, base64, 32 bytes.
#
#   openssl rand -base64 32
#
# That is the whole of it — 44 characters ending in `=`. Anything else is
# refused at start-up rather than used, because a key that is not a key would
# seal every credential under junk.
#
# Set this and nothing is written to disk — which is what a container or a
# server wants. Leave it unset and Firetower keeps one at ~/.firetower/root.key,
# mode 0600, created on first run. Either way it lives outside the database, so
# a database backup on its own opens nothing.
#
# Losing it means adding every credential again. Back it up separately.
# FIRETOWER_ROOT_KEY=

# Who to create as the administrator on a database with no users. Both
# optional: with neither, one is made and its password printed once at start-up.
# ADMIN_USERNAME=admin
# ADMIN_INITIAL_PASSWORD=

# Where Firetower keeps its state. Defaults to ~/.firetower
# FIRETOWER_HOME=

# The port the control plane listens on. Defaults to 4400
#
# `docker compose --profile full up` publishes on this one as well, so two
# checkouts can each run the whole stack without colliding.
# FIRETOWER_PORT=

# What that same stack publishes the interface on. Defaults to 3000. Only the
# containerised stack reads this — `just dev` runs the interface on the
# machine, where Next's own 3000 is the answer.
# WEB_PORT=3000

# The host address `docker compose --profile full` publishes the control plane
# and the interface on. Defaults to 127.0.0.1: the stack answers the machine
# running it and nothing else on the network.
#
# This is not FIRETOWER_BIND, and the two are often confused. That one is the
# address the control plane listens on *inside* its container, and compose.yaml
# sets it to 0.0.0.0 because a container listening on its own loopback
# publishes a port that answers nothing. This one is the host's side of the
# published mapping, and it is the one that decides who can reach the stack.
#
# Set it when the stack runs somewhere other than the machine you use it from
# — a VM, a spare box — to the address of the interface it should answer on:
#
#   HOST_BIND=192.168.1.20 WEB_PORT=3000 just up
#
# An address rather than 0.0.0.0, because the interface is told the same host
# to call the API on, and a browser cannot do anything with 0.0.0.0. What you
# are choosing is a development control plane without TLS — passwords and
# tokens in the clear — and Next's dev server, on that network. Worth doing
# deliberately; not worth defaulting to.
# HOST_BIND=127.0.0.1
